```java
// synchronized
class SyncCounter {
    private int count = 0;
    public synchronized void increment() { count++; }
    public synchronized int get() { return count; }
}

// explicit Lock
class LockCounter {
    private int count = 0;
    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    public void increment() {
        lock.lock();
        try { count++; } finally { lock.unlock(); }
    }
}

// atomic
class AtomicCounter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
    public void increment() { count.incrementAndGet(); }
}
```

Same invariant, three implementations. Note the `Lock` version's `try`/`finally` — unlocking must happen even if the body throws, or the lock leaks forever. The atomic version needs no explicit locking code; the safety lives inside the class.

Even the right individual tool isn't automatically enough. Two independent atomics don't protect an invariant spanning both:

```java
class Account {
    private final AtomicInteger balance = new AtomicInteger(100);
    private final AtomicInteger pending = new AtomicInteger(0);

    // BROKEN: each atomic op is safe alone, the pair isn't
    boolean withdraw(int amount) {
        if (balance.get() - pending.get() >= amount) {
            pending.addAndGet(amount);      // gap — another thread can slip in here
            balance.addAndGet(-amount);
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

The check and the two updates aren't one atomic operation, so two threads can both pass the check before either updates — overdrawing the account. The fix is a `synchronized` block (or a single `Lock`) around the whole check-and-update, not more atomics.
